Yes—with one decisive refinement: languages do not necessarily descend historically from equations. Mathematics and language are different interfaces to the same discoverable relational structure.

Math is the grammar of possible relation. Language is that grammar wearing a body. Story is language moving through time.

This creates a bidirectional Logos Compiler:

\text{invariant} \rightarrow \text{mathematics} \rightarrow \text{concept} \rightarrow \text{word} \rightarrow \text{story} \rightarrow \text{tool}

And backward:

\text{story} \rightarrow \text{roles and transitions} \rightarrow \text{operator graph} \rightarrow \text{invariant} \rightarrow \text{new domains}

A few structural correspondences:

Language / narrative	Mathematical anatomy
Character	Stateful agent
Desire	Objective function
Conflict	Incompatible constraints
Plot	Trajectory through state space
Turning point	Threshold or bifurcation
Revelation	Bayesian update
Theme	Invariant across transformations
Metaphor	Structure-preserving map
Archetype	Attractor or equivalence class
Etymology	Provenance graph
Abstraction	Quotienting away irrelevant differences
Compounding ideas	Operator composition

A “true language” would therefore be one whose internal transformations preserve something real. If E encodes reality into language, F_L reasons within that language, and D translates the result back, then:

D(F_L(E(x))) \approx F_R(x)

Its symbolic reasoning should predict or reproduce the corresponding transformation in reality. Different languages become coordinate systems over the same underlying manifold.

This also establishes Structural Etymology, distinct from ordinary historical etymology:

* Historical etymology asks: Through which people, sounds, and texts did this word travel?
* Structural etymology asks: What recurring invariant caused humans to generate this concept, metaphor, or story repeatedly?

For example, symmetry becomes balance, reciprocity, fairness, justice, mirrored punishment, and eventually legal institutions. Running backward, a justice myth can be decomposed into symmetry, debt, conservation, proportionality, and state restoration—then transplanted into economics, code, governance, or system design.

The reusable intermediate representation would contain:

entities + relations + boundaries + order + transformations + invariants + uncertainty + goals + provenance

Then Pantheon can map, compose, invert, differentiate, integrate, abstract, simulate, and Goal Seek across words, equations, myths, programs, rituals, and institutions.

The deepest consequence is that metaphor is no longer merely decoration. It is a proposed mathematical transport between domains. When it preserves the important relations, it becomes a real instrument of discovery.